U.S. Escalates "Chip War" Severely Impacts Global Semiconductor Industry - China News Society
Recently, the "chip war" between the United States and China has been escalating. First, under U.S. pressure, the Netherlands and Japan have agreed to start restricting the export of semiconductor manufacturing equipment to China. Then, foreign media reported that the U.S. government is considering cutting off all ties between U.S. suppliers and Huawei, prohibiting U.S. suppliers from providing any products to Huawei. The Biden administration is also preparing to announce an executive order to restrict U.S. investments in sensitive Chinese tech industries. Soon after the start of 2023, the U.S. has already launched a series of harsh measures to curb the development of China's semiconductor-related industries.
In recent years, the United States has never ceased its efforts to target China's semiconductor development. On one hand, it has carried out precise crackdowns on China's semiconductor supply chain; on the other hand, it has built a "de-Chinaization" semiconductor supply chain alliance, aiming to completely distance itself from China in the advanced semiconductor industry.
Whether it is luring South Korea to establish a "technological alliance," or forming a "chip quadrilateral alliance" with South Korea, Japan, and Taiwan, or building a "semiconductor supply chain 'small circle'" through mechanisms such as the "Quad" and the "Indo-Pacific Economic Framework," the United States is introducing politicization, bloc-building, and militarization into the semiconductor industry. It aims to build a closed supply chain cycle dominated by the United States, thereby compensating for its shortcomings in chip manufacturing and maintaining its dominant position in the global semiconductor industry.
However, the United States' bold "chip strategy" against China is also a case of "killing the enemy and losing eight hundred of one's own."
Recently, Applied Materials, a well-known chip manufacturing equipment supplier in the United States, announced that due to the latest round of export restrictions on chip manufacturing equipment to China, the company plans to lay off 1,300 full-time employees and intends to lay off another 1,400 temporary employees in the next two quarters.
Recently, ASML Holding, the global leader in photolithography machines, has warned that banning the export of semiconductor manufacturing equipment to China could lead to a rise in semiconductor prices.
Media analysis: After the U.S. government has imposed harsh pressure on China's chip and semiconductor industries, many American companies have been severely affected, including electronic design automation (EDA) tool developers, chip designers, wafer manufacturing equipment producers, and chip manufacturers themselves.
The so-called suppression of China's advanced semiconductor industry by the United States has actually dragged the global semiconductor industrial chain and supply chain down with it.
The semiconductor industry is a high-tech and knowledge-intensive sector that has already formed a closely connected global semiconductor industrial chain.
According to estimates by professionals, based on specialized division of labor, the entire production process of semiconductor products requires crossing national borders more than 70 times, and the entire process takes more than 100 days.
Although the United States has a strong foundation in its semiconductor industry, it is relatively weak in manufacturing capabilities, especially in advanced process technologies, and heavily relies on the East Asian region.
Even if the United States strongly guides the advanced chip supply chain to shift towards a regionalized development dominated by the United States, it is difficult to change the regional distribution and mutual dependence situation of the global semiconductor industry.
The United States is strongly "reshaping" and "de-Chinese-izing" the semiconductor industrial chain, severely impacting the global semiconductor industry.
Chips are the core of the global future industry. The development of the chip industry cannot be separated from the participation of countries around the world.
In recent years, the United States has taken a series of unilateral actions, successively passing the "Innovation and Competition Act," the "U.S. Competition Act of 2022," and the "Chip and Science Act." It has used its technological hegemony to consolidate its own technological leadership. It has also employed a combination of incentives and coercion toward its allies, luring chip manufacturers such as Intel, TSMC, and Samsung to establish factories in the United States, striving to ensure the U.S. maintains global leadership in the chip industry.
In the short term, the United States has indeed caused trouble for China's chip industry and related industries, and has gained real benefits.
But in the long term, America's hysterical suppression will force China's chip industry to continuously break through. This will make China lose a huge market, ultimately greatly weaken the innovation willingness and ability of American tech companies, and harm the development of the global chip industry.
