The fitness industry should escape the crisis of distrust - China News Society
Another major fitness gym has "collapsed"?
Recently, the issue of fitness club members seeking justice at the well-known fitness institution Zhongjian Fitness has drawn social attention.
After the Spring Festival, many members found that the stores they were affiliated with were not operating normally. Subsequently, rumors about the institution's collapse and disappearance caused panic among the members, leading to the involvement of public security and human resources and social security departments.
Finally, a relevant official from the institution stepped forward to respond, promising to strive to safeguard the legitimate rights and interests of employees and members, and the uproar was eventually calmed down.
Some branches of fitness institutions are experiencing operational problems and have temporarily closed. Consumers could calmly wait for the problem to be resolved. Why do many members' first reaction is "something bad is going to happen"?
The behind-the-scenes controversy involving fitness club members' rights protection reveals the deep-seated issues of trust crises in the industry.
Traditional fitness gyms at home generally adopt a prepayment model, and "making money from users who cannot be earned" is the fundamental logic of their development.
By pre-selling membership cards to generate cash flow and not opening new stores, it may seem like a recurring cycle, but in reality, it's like eating the rice of the next month to eat today's meal, and cannot form a stable profit model.
Frequent reports of negative information, such as gym closures, have severely shaken consumer confidence.
From the perspective of market prospects, fitness clubs certainly count as a profitable and promising business.
Although the fitness industry has experienced ups and downs, it has always attracted attention from capital.
Multiple data show that the enthusiasm for cultural and sports consumption is continuously rising, and the market remains steadily recovering.
Specifically in the fitness market, with the steady improvement of residents' living standards and the deep-rooted concept of a healthy lifestyle, the development of the fitness industry has great potential.
The better the situation becomes, the more the industry needs to focus on the quality of development.
However, some institutions are overly optimistic about the fitness market after the recovery of consumption. Having just emerged from the crisis, they have once again sounded the horn for expansion, pouring all their financing funds into the construction of new stores.
It is undeniable that in recent years, the impact of the pandemic has led to the exit of many institutions, and the industry has undergone a re-shuffling. It is not surprising that some capable enterprises have chosen to increase their acquisition efforts and reposition themselves at this time, which could be a new opportunity.
However, to enter a virtuous cycle and achieve a stable and lasting development in the fitness market, the industry must change its traditional model and adjust its business strategies.
Only by firmly establishing consumer trust and a sense of security can we truly win the market and the future.
Compared to building new stores to capture the market, the fitness industry currently more needs to focus on optimizing services and using high-quality development to stabilize users.
First, we need to rely on services to retain users.
GYM is part of the service industry, and its essence is to adhere to the "user-oriented" principle. It should closely revolve around user needs and place the focus of operations on improving and upgrading service quality.
Secondly, we need to rely on digital transformation to enhance user experience.
For example, the currently popular internet-based gym model allows users to pay per session and select their own facilities.
At the same time, body data and sports data can be effectively utilized to meet social needs and achieve a complete service loop from online to offline.
There is no personal trainer sales pitch, and no huge annual membership fees. As a result, users' concerns about fitness studios "disappearing" have decreased, and trust has increased.
Additionally, we should actively explore diverse business models.
Today, sports consumption has moved from a single service to scene-based consumption. Fitness clubs are expected to become new entry points for consumption. Around the sports supplies and fitness equipment needed for exercise, surrounding products may become new profit points, offering consumers more diverse choices.
