On the other hand, Google pointed out that Bard's response "highlighted the importance of rigorous testing processes." It will combine external feedback with its own internal testing to ensure that Bard's responses "meet high standards based on quality, safety, and factual accuracy."
Singapore's tourism industry is recovering strongly, with inbound tourists increasing 19 times compared to 2021.
(Xinhua, Nov. 11) The Singapore Tourism Board recently announced that the number of international visitors to Singapore is expected to reach 12 million to 14 million in 2023, bringing about tourism revenue of approximately S$18 billion to S$21 billion; the number of visitors and tourism revenue are expected to recover to about 67% to 75% of the levels before the pandemic in 2019. There is a high likelihood that the tourism industry will fully recover in 2024.
The Singapore tourism market gradually resumed and recovered in 2022.
According to the latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourist Authority, the number of inbound tourists in 2022 increased 19 times compared to 2021, rising from 3.306 million to 6.3 million. This exceeded the authority's earlier forecast of 4 to 6 million. Tourists have already recovered to 33% of pre-pandemic levels.
From January to September 2022, Singapore's tourism revenue reached 8.96 billion Singapore dollars. It is estimated that the total tourism revenue for the entire year of 2022 will reach 13.8 to 14.3 billion Singapore dollars, a growth of 6 to 7 times compared to the 1.89 billion Singapore dollars in 2021. This has already recovered to about 50% to 52% of the level before the pandemic in 2019.
The Singapore government is taking multiple measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism industry.
In April 2022, the government announced a nearly 500 million new dollar funding allocation to stimulate the recovery of the tourism industry.
The tourism bureau announced recently that it plans to allocate 110 million new dollars from this budget over the next two years to launch more business and leisure activities to attract tourists to return.
To make healthy living a key attraction of Singapore's tourism, the Singapore Tourism Board launched an expression of interest campaign on November 23, 2022, planning to develop a health and wellness destination along the southern coast of Singapore.
Wang Lingli, Director of the Singapore Tourist Board's Office for Sports and Wellness Tourism, said that in recent years, an increasing number of tourists have placed their health and well-being at the top of their consideration factors.
Developing a health and wellness attraction in the滨海南 area, which includes the Gardens by the Bay and the Marina Bay Sands, is expected to contribute to Singapore's tourism and economy by encouraging tourists to spend more time and money in the country.
Chen Jianlong, the Director of Tourism for Singapore, said that health and wellness will become an important component of Singapore's tourism products in the coming years.
The COVID-19 pandemic has increased public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
For this purpose, the tourism bureau has been promoting health and wellness programs to both local residents and international tourists over the past two years.
The Singapore Tourism Board held the first Healthy Living Festival in June 2022, offering the public over 130 health-related activities and experiences.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences for tourists.
The Tourism Board will support the development and updates of projects such as the Avatar World at Gardens by the Bay, the Sentosa Central Beach Boardwalk, the newly added "Exploring New Frontiers" exhibition at the National Museum of Singapore, and the outdoor theater at the Night Safari, as well as the Bird Park at Wild Asia, to make Singapore an attractive destination for international travelers.
According to the plan, Singapore will establish industry standards related to the sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions, and incentive tourism within the year 2023. The focus will be on improving waste management and carbon emissions, further optimizing the tourism environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
According to the data, as of December 18, 2022, the passenger traffic at Changi Airport has shown a strong recovery. The airport welcomed as many as 150,000 passengers per day, totaling about 10 million passengers per week, which is roughly 75% of the level before the pandemic. The number of aviation industry employees on the job has also recovered to about 90% of the pre-pandemic level.
The data shows that from January to October 2022, Singapore received 23.6 million tourists, equivalent to 42% of pre-pandemic levels, and handled 1.7 million flight takeoffs and landings, equivalent to 53% of pre-pandemic levels.
The rapid recovery of the tourism industry has also accelerated the recovery of sectors such as accommodation, transportation, catering, and shopping.
The Singapore government and the tourism industry are optimistic about the Chinese market.
Co Suhu, deputy director of the International Department of the Singapore Tourist Board, said that a survey by a Chinese travel platform showed that Singapore's safety index ranks among the top three globally, which is also one of the reasons why Singapore is popular among Chinese tourists.
Before the outbreak of the pandemic, about 3.6 million Chinese people traveled to Singapore for vacation each year.
With China easing its border policies, it is expected that the number of Chinese tourists this year will recover to 30% to 60% of that in 2019.
Ke Shudan pointed out that next, the Tourism Bureau will closely monitor the signals of further policy releases by China regarding outbound travel, to respond to changes in tourist demand.
The Singapore Tourism Board will not only strengthen partnerships with online travel platforms and travel agencies such as Ctrip in China, but also actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists, taking into account the new trends in outbound tourism among Chinese travelers abroad.
Looking ahead to the 2023 tourism market, the Singapore Tourism Board is confident that the recovery of the tourism industry will be faster than expected, provided that the global economy does not experience a significant downturn affecting public income, and there are no new large-scale deadly pandemic outbreaks.
