On Friday (February 10), the U.S. military again used its most advanced stealth jet, the F-22, to shoot down an unidentified object flying over Alaska.
The location where the plane was shot down was ten miles off the Alaskan coast.
This is the second time the U.S. military has downed an aerial object within less than a week.
Last Saturday, a spy balloon from China drifted over U.S. airspace for several days and was destroyed near the coast of South Carolina in the United States.
The Pentagon said that the second object was first entered U.S. airspace on Thursday night. It was tracked by U.S. military forces as it moved over the northern part of Alaska, flying at an altitude of 12,000 meters (40,000 feet). The object was about the size of a small car and was much smaller than the previous Chinese spy balloon that was shot down.
The Pentagon also believes there is no indication currently that the object is controllable.
White House National Security Council spokesperson John Kirby said on Friday that although the exact appearance of the object flying at high altitude is unclear, it "poses a reasonable threat to the safety of commercial aircraft."
Kobe said that this object is different from the spy balloon found in the United States, as it does not carry a "payload."
Kobe said, "We call it an 'object'—it is the best description we can currently come up with."
Biden listened to advice from the Pentagon before ordering the missile strike, and he made the decision "very carefully," said科比.
The official description of this object in the United States can be roughly summarized as: it is about the size of a car, has no steering wheel, no suspension system, and flies at an altitude of around 10,000 meters.
