I read it a long time ago. I happened to see it today. Let me write down some thoughts based on my memory.
This book is more of a collection of fragments about love in life, rather than a collection of short stories.
The tone of this book is extremely plain, like plain water, and the language is simplified to its utmost extent. The plot is as quiet and ordinary as the streets on a summer afternoon.
Each short story is a snapshot of a brief moment in ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally, they were collected together and named "What Are We Talking About When We Talk About Love?"
I think this collection of short stories and its title are like a form of performance art. The author wants to convey that love is not just the tragic and romantic scenes on stage, not just the eternal vows in urban legends, nor just the sweet and clingy love that's inseparable. Love is not just these things, and it should not be represented by them. Love is also the ordinary and quiet life, the monotonous day after day, and also the helplessness and confusion in facing life, the escape from reality, and the uncertainty about the future.
When you read this book, you may imagine the arguing couple next to you, the dull marriages of people you know, and unsatisfactory relationships.
This is exactly why I don't recommend reading this book. From an aesthetic perspective, the writing and plot are dull and uninteresting. Unless your life is full of dramatic ups and downs or as sweet as living in a candy castle, you will feel indifferent and bored while reading it.
