"I appreciate you, but I want you to know more!"
The first factor that determines a person's job satisfaction is not the level of salary, but whether they are given enough attention and recognition in their work.
According to Maslow's hierarchy of needs theory, after basic survival needs are met, the most urgent need is the satisfaction of psychological needs—feeling understood, appreciated, and recognized.
Indeed, for the Chinese people, most of them feel "I love you, but I can't say it out loud." Even if they have love, gratitude, and admiration, they tend to keep these feelings in their hearts and never express them verbally.
The author presents five languages of appreciation in this book: words of affirmation, quality time, acts of service, gifts that please, and physical touch.
What's important is not that you know exactly what the five languages of appreciation are, but that you can genuinely use these five languages to appreciate and be grateful for people around you at any time, whether they are family members, friends, or colleagues.
Actually, the closer the relationship, the more we need to appreciate and be grateful to each other. This will make our lives more beautiful.
Learning to appreciate employees can help companies retain talent to a certain extent and reduce turnover rates.
Thank you to the translator for bringing us the fascinating ideas from abroad and the world.
Due to differences in cultural backgrounds between the East and the West, languages will inevitably have some differences. If we have the opportunity to see the original version, we would probably gain more insights.
Once again, thank you to the authors, translators, and editors for their hard work!
